title: Add opt-in app-server-backed Codex session launcher
---
Depends on P1.M1.E1.T001 proving the protocol.

Only if the T001 control-boundary gate passes, make canonical `c2c start codex` launch an app-server behind that proven boundary and run interactive Codex as `codex --remote <validated endpoint>`. Its default alias must be generated from the stable Codex/app-server session identity; `--alias` is an optional human-selected override, never a default.

Acceptance criteria:
- Enforce the T001-proven boundary. A private Unix socket location or mode is not sufficient on a same-UID swarm host.
- An unauthorized same-UID probe cannot connect or invoke `turn/start`, `turn/steer`, `turn/interrupt`, shell, filesystem, or other app-server controls. Test this in the launcher E2E receipt.
- Endpoint lifecycle, cleanup, restart behavior, and secret/mapping storage are deterministic; never persist an access credential in a peer-readable location.
- Never expose a TCP listener by default; a non-local endpoint requires explicit operator configuration and documented authentication.
- A normal Codex TUI exit stops its app-server and marks the managed alias offline. It must not leave an unattended worker running in this normal lifecycle.
- Support explicit `--yolo` as a transparent forwarding alias for Codex `--dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox`; it is never implicit and must be visible in instance/status output.
- Clearly report unavailable/unsupported Codex versions.
- The launcher has fixture-gated tests plus a real tmux dogfood launch.

Blockers: P1.M1.E1.T001 must produce a positive control-boundary go decision as well as protocol observations. If it is no-go, keep this task blocked and retain hooks as the supported path.
